//
// A NAME SPELLED AFTER ITS BINDING'S SCOPE ENDED STARTS A NEW BINDING, AT ANY TYPE (`@FR-B-Scope`).
// A local bound inside a block ends at the block's `}`, a loop variable ends with its loop and a
// comprehension's variable ends with the comprehension; a later bind of the same spelling — a
// plain local, a loop variable, a value from a function defined further down — is a new binding
// with its own type, never the ended one reused.  Each spelling once leaked the ended binding:
// refused as a type change or a shadow, read with the ended one's type, or built through the
// loop variable's view into the walked collection.  Six sections, each with its cells and
// receipt: after a block (loft#1700), with the value from a later function (loft#2411), after a
// loop at the same type (loft#2332), a loop variable after a block (loft#2321), a later local
// beside a comprehension's variable (loft#2330), and a body local spelled again in a sibling loop.
//
// ── 1700-a-bind-after-its-block-ended-is-a-new-binding-at-any-type ──
//
// loft#1700 (`@FR-B-Scope`) — A BIND AFTER ITS BLOCK ENDED IS A NEW BINDING, AT ANY TYPE.
// `(B-Scope)` ends a local bound inside a block at the block's `}`, and a bind after it starts a
// new binding in the enclosing block.  The parser kept one variable per name, so the new binding
// inherited the ended one's type and `if c { w = 1 } w = "x"` was refused as a type change.  A
// bind after the block ended, at a type the ended binding refuses, now gets a variable of its
// own, as a `for` loop's and a pattern's do; the spelling names it until the enclosing block
// ends.  Only where the old compiler refused (loft#1145's rule), so a program that compiled is
// unchanged — a same-type rebind keeps its single binding and its lints.
//
// The closure capturing a spelling several bindings share is
// `1700b-a-closure-captures-the-binding-its-name-spells-now.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: 5ba2b5ef5 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1700-a-bind-after-its-block-ended-is-a-new-binding-at-any-type.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
// CHANNEL: the exit — the control tree refuses this file ("cannot change type").
// ARMED: every run.
// WITNESS: `Variable 'w' cannot change type from integer to text`.
// HOLDS: the same-type rebind and the read-after-end refusal (the corpus's B-Scope guards).
//
// ── 2411-a-bind-from-a-later-function-after-its-block-ended-is-a-new-binding ──
//
// loft#2411 (`@FR-B-Scope`) — A BIND FROM A LATER FUNCTION, AFTER ITS BLOCK ENDED, IS A NEW
// BINDING.  The same rule as `1700-a-bind-after-its-block-ended-is-a-new-binding-at-any-type.loft`,
// with the value coming from a call to a function defined further down: the first pass has no
// type for it yet, and it read the new binding with the ended one's type — `r >= 0` refused on
// a vector, the compile stopping before the second pass could split the binding.  A local whose
// latest first-pass bind has no type yet now reads as untyped there, as a fresh one would; a
// declared local keeps its declaration (its last two cells, and the text cell, held before).
//
// @falsified-at: 369b941cf — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
// CHANNEL: the exit — the control refuses this file ("No matching operator").
// ARMED: every run.
// WITNESS: `No matching operator '>=' on 'integer' and 'vector<integer>'`.
// HOLDS: `texts`, `same`, `declared` (a text read of the ended vector, a same-type rebind, a
// declared local).
//
// ── 2332-a-bind-after-a-loop-is-a-new-binding-at-the-loop-variables-type ──
//
// loft#2332 — `@FR-B-Scope`: a loop variable ends with its loop, so a bind of its spelling after
// the loop starts a NEW binding — also at the SAME type.  The bind reused the loop's variable,
// which views an element of the walked collection: the new value was built through that view into
// the collection (`top` answered t=3 for 6, the second element overwritten), inside an enclosing
// loop the view became an owner that freed the walked store every pass (`nested`, a use after free
// under LOFT_STRICT_STORES), and rebound in a later loop the interpreter hung (`later_loop`).
//
// Every value by hand.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  where the later bind sits (the function's block, an enclosing loop's
//                        body, a later loop's body), its form (plain, declared), whether pass 1
//                        knows the value's type (a function declared before, after), the loop
//                        variable's kind (a record view, text, integer, destructured binders),
//                        backends.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   one loop over one collection before the bind; the collection a field.
//
// @falsified-at: 0e95172cd — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
// CHANNEL: the assertions — main answers `top 3 3` on both backends.
//   HOLDS: the leak check and the panic channel are clean on BOTH trees, on the
//   interpreter and the C (measured on main 0e95172cd unarmed, 2026-11-02); only the
//   exit moves.
//
// ── 2321-a-loop-variable-over-an-ended-blocks-name-is-a-new-binding ──
//
// loft#2321 — `@FR-B-Scope`: a local bound inside a block ends at its `}`, so a loop variable — a
// `for` statement's or a comprehension's — spelled like it after that block, or in a sibling arm,
// is a NEW binding, of any type.  It was refused: the statement loop as a shadow ("a loop variable
// cannot reuse the name of a local still in scope"), the comprehension as a type change reported
// on the ended local's own bind — the loop's variable kept the spelling after its body, so the
// second pass met the earlier arm's `t = "a"` with `t` already naming the loop's variable.
//
// Every value by hand.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  the loop (statement, comprehension), where the ended local sits (the
//                        other arm of an `if`, a bare block before, an `if` before), the two
//                        types (equal, different), the loop variable's kind (integer, text,
//                        a record), the order (the local first, the loop first), backends.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   one function per cell; the local a plain statement bind.
//
// @falsified-at: 0e95172cd — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
// CHANNEL: the compile — main refuses this file.
//   HOLDS: the leak check and the panic channel are clean on BOTH trees, on the
//   interpreter and the C (measured on main 0e95172cd unarmed, 2026-11-02); only the
//   exit moves.
//
// ── 2330-a-comprehension-variable-keeps-its-type-beside-a-later-local-of-its-name ──
//
// `@FR-B-Scope` — a comprehension's loop variable ends with the comprehension, so a LATER
// local spelled like it is a new binding with its own type.  The comprehension variable
// took that later binding's type instead: a later `c = cs[end]` (a `character?`, an
// element read) re-typed the comprehension's `c` to `character?`, and `@FR-N-Store` then
// warned that a nullable `character?` "is stored into the comprehension element of the
// non-null type `character`" — a warning, the tier that gates a library's CI, about a
// value that is never null (loft#2330).
//
// Armed with `LOFT_DENY_WARNINGS=1`, so the spurious warning fails the cell; each function
// asserts its hand-computed answer too, so a variable that stopped warning while reading
// the wrong binding fails as well.  The axes: where the later local sits (a `while` body,
// the function's own block, a `for` body, BEFORE the comprehension in an ended block),
// the later local's type (the same nullable, `integer?`, `text`), a filtered comprehension,
// and two comprehensions spelling one name.
//
// @ARMED: LOFT_DENY_WARNINGS=1
// @falsified-at: 0e95172cd (loft#2330) — interpret and native-c: three unexpected warnings
// (`later_in_while`, `filtered`, `two_comprehensions`), the cell refused under deny-warnings.
//   CHANNEL: the false warning, made the exit by LOFT_DENY_WARNINGS=1.
//   WITNESS: main warns `a nullable `character?` is stored into the comprehension
//   element` three times (the while, filtered and two-comprehension shapes).
//   HOLDS: the leak check and the panic channel are clean on BOTH trees, on the
//   interpreter and the C (measured on main 0e95172cd unarmed, 2026-11-02); only the
//   exit moves.
//
// ── a-name-bound-in-sibling-loops-is-one-binding-per-loop-on-every-backend ──
//
// `@FR-B-Scope` — A NAME BOUND IN SIBLING LOOPS IS ONE BINDING PER LOOP, ON EVERY BACKEND.
// `ops: vector<Nd> = […]` in one `for` body and again in the next spells a new binding each
// time: the earlier one's scope has ended.  The scope pass makes each a variable of its own,
// and runs twice where a vector store is confined to a block; the second run registered the
// local at its stores' common block when the local's one dep named ANOTHER store (the last
// loop's empty literal), so it never split, and one variable spanned all the loops.  The
// interpreter and the C read that fine, but `--native` declares a local at its first write:
// `var_ops` existed in the first loop only and rustc refused the second (E0425) — the program
// did not build.  Here the match is on a LOCAL whose arm result is copied into the return
// buffer, which is what puts the stores' null-inits inside the arm.  loft-in-loft's
// elaborator (@PLN91) has this shape in `finish_body`.
//
// The expected list is worked out by hand: `[5]`, each loop prepends what it builds, the last
// inserts `1007` before position 0.
//
// @falsified-at: 309999235 — native (FALSIFY_NATIVE=rust) exit 1 -> 0; interpret INERT (expected
//   for a backend-divergence guard — only one side can move)
//   CHANNEL: the exit code on `--native` (a rustc E0425; no program runs); the interpreter and
//   the C agree before and after.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic and refusals are unmoved on both trees.

// The filed shape: the later local in a `while` body.  "a(b": cs = [a,(,b]; end 0 -> 1,
// end 1 '(' -> 3; 3 < 3 stops.

enum E { A, B }

fn test_a_bind_after_its_block_ended_is_a_new_binding_at_any_type() {
  c = true;
  if c { w = 1; assert(w == 1, "the first binding"); }
  w = "x";
  assert(w == "x", "a new binding at another type: {w}");
  t = 0;
  for e in [1, 2] { t += e; }
  e = "e";
  assert(e == "e" && t == 3, "after a loop variable: {e} {t}");
  {
    if c { d = 1; assert(d == 1, "nested first"); }
    d = "d";
    assert(d == "d", "inside a nested block: {d}");
  }
  if c { i1 = 1; assert(i1 == 1, "i1"); }
  if c { i1 = "i"; assert(i1 == "i", "rebound inside another block: {i1}"); }
  ev = E.A;
  match ev { A => { m = 1; assert(m == 1, "arm body"); }, B => {} }
  m = "m";
  assert(m == "m", "after a match arm's local: {m}");
  if c { h = 1; assert(h == 1, "h"); }
  h: text = "h";
  assert(h == "h", "a declared new binding: {h}");
  if c { j = [1, 2]; assert(len(j) == 2, "a heap binding"); }
  j = "j";
  assert(j == "j", "a heap binding ended, a text one started: {j}");
  n = 0;
  for i in 0..3 { if c { l = i; n += l; } l = [i, i]; n += len(l); }
  assert(n == 9, "a scalar then a vector per iteration: {n}");
  if c { k = 1; assert(k == 1, "k"); }
  k = "k";
  f = fn(s: text) -> text { "{k}{s}" };
  assert(f("y") == "ky", "a closure capturing the new binding: {f("y")}");
  // unchanged: the same type keeps one binding
  if c { s = 1; assert(s == 1, "s"); }
  s = 2;
  assert(s == 2, "the same type: {s}");
  println("a bind after its block ended is a new binding at any type: every cell holds");
}

fn ops(c: boolean) -> integer {
  if c { r = [1, 2]; assert(len(r) == 2, "the first binding"); }
  r = later_int(37);
  if r >= 0 { r + 5 } else { -1 }
}

fn items(c: boolean) -> vector<integer> {
  if c { r = "ended"; assert(r == "ended", "the first binding"); }
  r = later_int(11);
  [r, r * 2]
}

fn texts(c: boolean) -> text {
  if c { v = [3]; assert(len(v) == 1, "the first binding"); }
  v = later_text("ab");
  "{v}{v + "c"}"
}

fn looped(n: integer) -> integer {
  t = 0;
  for i in 0..n {
    if i > 0 { q = [i]; t += len(q); }
    q = later_int(i);
    t += q * 10;
  }
  t
}

fn same(c: boolean) -> integer {
  if c { s = 4; assert(s == 4, "the first binding"); }
  s = later_int(6);
  s * 3
}

fn declared(c: boolean) -> integer {
  d: integer = later_int(8);
  if c { d = later_int(9); }
  d + 1
}

fn later_int(n: integer) -> integer { n }
fn later_text(s: text) -> text { s }

fn test_a_bind_from_a_later_function_after_its_block_ended_is_a_new_binding() {
  assert(ops(true) == 42, "an operator on the new binding: {ops(true)}");
  assert(ops(false) == 42, "the block not taken: {ops(false)}");
  assert(items(true) == [11, 22], "a vector of the new binding: {items(true)}");
  assert(texts(true) == "ababc", "a text new binding: {texts(true)}");
  assert(looped(3) == 32, "per iteration: {looped(3)}");
  assert(same(true) == 18, "the same type keeps one binding: {same(true)}");
  assert(declared(true) == 10, "a declared local: {declared(true)}");
  println("a bind from a later function after its block ended is a new binding: every cell holds");
}

struct S3A { n: text, k: integer }
struct D { attrs: vector<S3A>, name: text }
struct S3E { defs: vector<D>, log: vector<text> }

fn mk(n: text) -> S3A { S3A { n: n, k: len(n) } }
fn def(e: S3E, d: integer) -> D { e.defs[d]! }

fn ksum(d: D) -> integer {
  t = 0;
  for x in d.attrs { t += x.k; }
  t
}

// 1: the bind in the function's own block, after a walk of the field it appends to
fn top() {
  d = D { attrs: [S3A { n: "p", k: 1 }, S3A { n: "qq", k: 2 }], name: "d" };
  s = 0;
  for a in d.attrs { s += a.k; }
  a = mk("rrr");
  d.attrs += [a];
  assert(s == 3 && ksum(d) == 6 && len(d.attrs) == 3, "top {s} {ksum(d)}");
  assert(d.attrs[1].n == "qq" && d.attrs[2].n == "rrr", "top elements");
}

// 2: inside an enclosing loop, the walk through a call returning the record
fn add_fields(e: S3E, d: integer, names: vector<text>) {
  for fname in names {
    for a in def(e, d).attrs {
      if a.n == fname {
        e.log += ["dup {fname}"];
      }
    }
    a = mk(fname);
    e.defs[d].attrs += [a];
  }
}
fn nested() {
  e = S3E { defs: [D { attrs: [], name: "d" }], log: [] };
  add_fields(e, 0, ["x", "yy", "x", "zzz"]);
  assert(len(e.defs[0].attrs) == 4 && ksum(e.defs[0]) == 7, "nested {ksum(e.defs[0])}");
  assert(len(e.log) == 1 && e.log[0] == "dup x", "nested log {e.log}");
}

// 3: a s3_declared bind
fn s3_declared() {
  d = D { attrs: [S3A { n: "p", k: 1 }], name: "d" };
  s = 0;
  for a in d.attrs { s += a.k; }
  a: S3A = mk("rrr");
  d.attrs += [a];
  assert(s == 1 && ksum(d) == 4 && d.attrs[0].n == "p", "s3_declared {s} {ksum(d)}");
}

// 4: the value from a function s3_declared further down, so pass 1 does not know its type
fn forward() {
  d = D { attrs: [S3A { n: "p", k: 1 }], name: "d" };
  s = 0;
  for a in d.attrs { s += a.k; }
  a = mk_late("rrrr");
  d.attrs += [a];
  assert(s == 1 && ksum(d) == 5 && d.attrs[0].n == "p", "forward {s} {ksum(d)}");
}

// 5: the bind in a later loop's body
fn later_loop() {
  d = D { attrs: [S3A { n: "p", k: 1 }], name: "d" };
  s = 0;
  for a in d.attrs { s += a.k; }
  for y in ["uu", "vvv"] {
    a = mk(y);
    d.attrs += [a];
  }
  assert(s == 1 && ksum(d) == 6 && len(d.attrs) == 3 && d.attrs[0].n == "p", "later {s} {ksum(d)}");
}

// 6: a text loop variable, then a text bind that grows
fn text_var() {
  names = ["ab", "cde"];
  s = 0;
  for t in names { s += len(t); }
  t = "xyzw";
  t += "!";
  assert(s == 5 && t == "xyzw!" && names[0] == "ab" && names[1] == "cde", "text {s} {t}");
}

// 7: an integer loop variable
fn int_var() {
  s = 0;
  for i in 0..3 { s += i; }
  i = 50;
  i += 1;
  assert(s == 3 && i == 51, "int {s} {i}");
}

// 8: destructured binders
fn destructured() {
  v = [(1, "ab"), (2, "cde")];
  s = 0;
  for (n, t) in v { s += n + len(t); }
  t = "xy";
  t += "z";
  n = 40;
  assert(s == 8 && t == "xyz" && n == 40 && v[1].1 == "cde", "destructured {s} {t} {n}");
}

fn mk_late(n: text) -> S3A { S3A { n: n, k: len(n) } }

fn test_a_bind_after_a_loop_is_a_new_binding_at_the_loop_variables_type() {
  top();
  nested();
  s3_declared();
  forward();
  later_loop();
  text_var();
  int_var();
  destructured();
}

struct P { n: integer }

// 1: a text local in the `if` arm, an integer `for` in the `else` arm
fn arms_for(k: integer) -> text {
  r = "";
  if k == 1 { t = "a"; r = t; } else { n = 0; for t in [1, 2] { n += t; } r = "{n}"; }
  r
}
// 2: the same with a comprehension
fn arms_comp(k: integer) -> text {
  r = "";
  if k == 1 { t = "a"; r = t; } else { v = [for t in [1, 2] { t * 2 }]; r = "{len(v)}:{v[1]}"; }
  r
}
// 3: equal types, a statement loop
fn arms_same(k: integer) -> integer {
  r = 0;
  if k == 1 { t = 5; r = t; } else { for t in [1, 2] { r += t; } }
  r
}
// 4: a bare block before a loop over text
fn block_then_for() -> text {
  r = "";
  { t = 7; r = "{t}"; }
  for t in ["x", "y"] { r += t; }
  r
}
// 5: an `if` before a comprehension over records
fn if_then_comp(k: integer) -> integer {
  r = 0;
  if k > 0 { t = "a"; r = len(t); }
  v = [for t in [P { n: 3 }, P { n: 4 }] { t.n * 10 }];
  r + v[0] + v[1]
}
// 6: the loop first, the local after (the order that always compiled)
fn loop_then_local() -> text {
  n = 0;
  for t in [1, 2] { n += t; }
  t = "z";
  "{n}{t}"
}
// 7: the ended local read after the loop is still out of reach; a bind there is a new one
fn bind_after(k: integer) -> text {
  r = "";
  if k == 1 { t = "a"; r = t; }
  v = [for t in [4] { t }];
  t = v[0] * 2;
  "{r}{t}"
}

fn test_a_loop_variable_over_an_ended_blocks_name_is_a_new_binding() {
  o1 = "{arms_for(1)}|{arms_for(2)}";
  assert(o1 == "a|3", "c1 {o1}");
  o2 = "{arms_comp(1)}|{arms_comp(2)}";
  assert(o2 == "a|2:4", "c2 {o2}");
  o3 = "{arms_same(1)}|{arms_same(2)}";
  assert(o3 == "5|3", "c3 {o3}");
  o4 = block_then_for();
  assert(o4 == "7xy", "c4 {o4}");
  o5 = "{if_then_comp(1)}|{if_then_comp(0)}";
  assert(o5 == "71|70", "c5 {o5}");
  o6 = loop_then_local();
  assert(o6 == "3z", "c6 {o6}");
  o7 = "{bind_after(1)}|{bind_after(2)}";
  assert(o7 == "a8|8", "c7 {o7}");
  println("ok");
}

fn later_in_while(t: text) -> integer {
  cs: vector<character> = [for c in t { c }];
  end = 0;
  while end < len(cs) {
    c = cs[end];
    if c == '(' { end += 1; }
    end += 1;
  }
  end
}

// The later local in the function's own block.  cs[1] is 'x' -> 10.
fn later_at_top(t: text) -> integer {
  cs: vector<character> = [for c in t { c }];
  c = cs[1];
  if c == 'x' { 10 } else { len(cs) }
}

// Over a vector<integer>, the later local an `integer?`.  [2, 4, 6] sums to 12.
fn later_integer(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  ds: vector<integer> = [for c in v { c * 2 }];
  s = 0;
  for i in 0..len(ds) {
    c = ds[i];
    if c != null { s += c; }
  }
  s
}

// The later local of another type.  "ax" and "bx": 2 + 2.
fn s5_later_text(t: text) -> integer {
  cs: vector<character> = [for c in t { c }];
  n = 0;
  for k in 0..len(cs) {
    c = "{cs[k]}x";
    n += len(c);
  }
  n
}

// The nullable local BEFORE the comprehension, in a block that has ended.  t[1] is 'b' ->
// 100, plus three characters.
fn earlier_ended(t: text) -> integer {
  n = 0;
  if len(t) > 1 {
    c = t[1];
    if c == 'b' { n += 100; }
  }
  cs: vector<character> = [for c in t { c }];
  n + len(cs)
}

// A filtered comprehension.  "abz" keeps [b, z]; m 0 -> 1, m 1 'z' -> 2 + 5 = 7.
fn filtered(t: text) -> integer {
  cs: vector<character> = [for c in t if c != 'a' { c }];
  m = 0;
  while m < len(cs) {
    c = cs[m];
    m += 1;
    if c == 'z' { m += 5; }
  }
  m
}

// Two comprehensions spelling one name.  "pq": k 0 'p' -> 1, k 1 'q' -> 12; 12 + 2 = 14.
fn two_comprehensions(t: text) -> integer {
  a: vector<character> = [for c in t { c }];
  b: vector<character> = [for c in t { c }];
  k = 0;
  while k < len(b) {
    c = a[k];
    if c == 'q' { k += 10; }
    k += 1;
  }
  k + len(a)
}

fn test_a_comprehension_variable_keeps_its_type_beside_a_later_local_of_its_na() {
  assert(later_in_while("a(b") == 3, "later_in_while {later_in_while("a(b")}");
  assert(later_at_top("axb") == 10, "later_at_top {later_at_top("axb")}");
  assert(later_integer([1, 2, 3]) == 12, "later_integer {later_integer([1, 2, 3])}");
  assert(s5_later_text("ab") == 4, "s5_later_text {s5_later_text("ab")}");
  assert(earlier_ended("abc") == 103, "earlier_ended {earlier_ended("abc")}");
  assert(filtered("abz") == 7, "filtered {filtered("abz")}");
  assert(two_comprehensions("pq") == 14, "two_comprehensions {two_comprehensions("pq")}");
}

// ── a name bound in sibling loops: one binding per loop ──

enum Nd {
  NdInt { n: integer },
  NdSet { v: integer, x: Nd },
  NdBlock { block: vector<Bl> },
}

struct Bl {
  name: text,
  operators: vector<Nd>,
}

struct At {
  at: integer,
  r: integer,
}

fn sh(n: Nd) -> text {
  match n {
    NdInt { n } => "{n}",
    NdSet { v } => "s{v}",
    _ => "b"
  }
}

fn ident(n: Nd) -> Nd {
  n
}

fn fin(body: Nd, xs: vector<integer>, ys: vector<integer>) -> Nd {
  code = if len(xs) > 5 { body } else { ident(body) };
  match code {
    NdBlock { block } => {
      bl = block[0];
      for p in xs {
        ops: vector<Nd> = [NdSet { v: p, x: NdInt { n: 0 } }, NdInt { n: p + 100 }];
        for op in bl.operators { ops += [op]; }
        bl.operators = ops;
      }
      for q in ys {
        ops: vector<Nd> = [NdInt { n: q }];
        for op in bl.operators { ops += [op]; }
        bl.operators = ops;
      }
      places: vector<At> = [];
      for r in ys {
        places += [At { at: 0, r: r }];
      }
      for x in places {
        ops: vector<Nd> = [];
        for op in bl.operators {
          if op#index == x.at { ops += [NdInt { n: 1000 + x.r }]; }
          ops += [op];
        }
        bl.operators = ops;
      }
      NdBlock { block: [bl] }
    },
    _ => code
  }
}

fn test_a_name_bound_in_sibling_loops_is_one_binding_per_loop() {
  r = fin(NdBlock { block: [Bl { name: "b", operators: [NdInt { n: 5 }] }] }, [1, 2], [7]);
  res = "";
  match r {
    NdBlock { block } => { for o in block[0].operators { res += sh(o) + ","; } },
    _ => {}
  }
  assert(res == "1007,7,s2,102,s1,101,5,", "each loop's own `ops`: {res}");
}
